Ahh, ok, I think understand the problem: GCC is generating gimple with 64 bit pointers, instead of 32 bits.

GCC-Bridge uses JVM arrays of primitives when a "real" arrays are manipulated with pointer arithmetic and so we are limited to 32-bit indexing.

Normally, when compiling on a 64 bit system like mine, Renjin will ask GCC <https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/tools/gcc-bridge/src/main/java/org/renjin/gcc/Gcc.java#L69> to cross-compile to 32-bits with the -m32 argument. But when I look at your maven build log, maven reports that "os.arch=i386"

Is your physical machine 32-bit or 64-bit? Is it possible that you're running a 32-bit JVM on a 64-bit machine? Or are you running a VM to build renjin and Java is getting confused ?

-Alex

On Fri, Nov 21, 2014 at 2:30 PM, Adriano Caloiaro <adr...@caloiaro.com> wrote:

> Correction: I found the problem in the jimple, but the question is why my system would generate different jimple than yours. The problem is with a cast from long to int on line 117 (and 167). To be more specific, the cast is occurring because the variable in the precious line (116) is being treated as a long rather than an int: T2659 = T2658 * 8L;  . When these casts are removed, the jimple will parse. However, it does introduce a new RuntimeException, presumably because the stack is sized incorrectly as a result of my hand-editing the jimple, yielding the following RuntimeException: StackHeight: -1 from the fill_array function. So the solution lies in the jimple code generation, which I imagine is the product of some decision made farther up the chain; perhaps a decision made by my particular version of gcc when creating the gimple? I’ll take a look farther up the chain.
