From u9fec238e3bf8496b at groups.google.com  Fri Nov 21 20:53:44 2014
From: u9fec238e3bf8496b at groups.google.com (Adriano Caloiaro)
Date: Fri, 21 Nov 2014 20:53:44 +0000
Subject: New PRNG / Mersenne-Twister implementation for Renjin
Message-ID: <Dzj1N1iDvcY.a2W1IaXIm50J@renjin-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/renjin-dev/c/Dzj1N1iDvcY/m/a2W1IaXIm50J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

As stated by Alex Bertram ( https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/issues/75 ), it is known that Renjin's PRNG produces different random numbers than RNU R when given the same seed. In response, I have re-implemented GNU R's Mersenne-Twister ( RNG.c, the default PRNG ) in Java so that it can be incorporated in lieu of Apache Commons Math's.

Unit Tests/Expected Values:
Because many stats functions rely upon unif_rand(), testRNG.R's unit tests had to be updated. I've updated the expected values to GNU R's expected output. After making these updates, my implementation passes 18 out of 21 RNG unit tests with the following tests not passing: test.rcauchy, test.rhyper, and test.multinom. Given that these three functions rely upon unif_rand() and their previous expected values were, "not generated by the original interpreter" [Comment, TestRNG.R Line 3] I am unsure what to do about these tests.

I would like to solicit the community's opinion on the following items:
What is an acceptable level of assurance that my implementation is indeed 100% compatible with GNU R? I've validated it against 100,000 random numbers from GNU R's runif(). Source code with test: https://github.com/acaloiaro/mersenne-twister-gnur
What is the proper procedure for introducing a change that breaks existing unit tests? Should a contributor recuse oneself from writing tests for his or her own code?
What should be done about the three failing unit tests? Should these have ever passed? The previous existing values do not match my output or GNU R's.
Fork here: https://github.com/acaloiaro/renjin

Thanks for any feedback.

AC

