MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

No problem, Alex.

I'm also not an expert in this domain, but I agree with you that a comprehensive suite of statistical randomness tests would be ideal. Considering the degree to which R is employed in the statistical and scientific communities, correctness and rigor should be of great importance to Renjin. Unfortunately I will not have enough time to implement comprehensive randomness tests for this implementation. It should also be noted the GNU R's Mersenne-Twister is based on Matsumoto and Nishimura's initial Mersenne-Twister specification from 1998, and thus mine is as well. This version of Marsenne-Twister will pass most, but not all statistical randomness tests. For example, it will not pass all of the TestU01 tests ( http://simul.iro.umontreal.ca/testu01/tu01.html ), but it will pass most of them. This version of Mersenne-Twister should not be considered cryptographically secure and likely would not pass the proposed NIST suite of tests.

To address the statistical randomness problem from a different angle, I think there is a fundamental question to be asked. Is Renjin's objective to be at parity with GNU R, or is it to become a wholly independent R interpreter for the JVM that is unencumbered by cross-compatibility? If the former, I think the focus should be on simple I/O parity tests with GNU R. If the latter, I would recommend continuing to use Apache Commons' MersenneTwister implementation because it is likely faster than mine, given that it is not encumbered by the GNU R-compatibility constraint. This constraint mandates some seed-scrambling and a 64-bit to 32-bit seed conversion that will draw the performance down. In fact, if GNU R parity is not a primary objective, I would recommend a different default PRNG entirely. While MT is the most widely implemented RPNG, it is not the fastest and is not the most statistically random one available.

Personally I need GNU R compatibility, but I imagine there are plenty of people with no such requirement and they would simply prefer the best tools for the job. It's certainly a debate worth having.

